#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/11-oop-classes.md:957
msgid "7. Dataclasses"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/11-oop-classes.md:959
msgid "A class in a few lines"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/11-oop-classes.md:961
msgid "Most classes for data start with an `__init__` that only copies the parameters into attributes, and a `__repr__`. The module `dataclasses` writes them for you, from the **fields** with their type hints (session 4):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/11-oop-classes.md:986
msgid "`@dataclass` writes `__init__`, `__repr__` and `__eq__` (equal when all fields are equal). Methods and properties are written as in a normal class."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/11-oop-classes.md:989
msgid "Type hints are not checked"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/11-oop-classes.md:990
msgid "`Loan(\"L1\", \"x\", \"a lot\")` works: a dataclass does not check the types (like all type hints, session 4). Check what matters in `__post_init__` (below)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/11-oop-classes.md:994
msgid "A row of `csv.DictReader` can go straight into a dataclass with `**` (session 4) - if the column names are the field names. Convert the types in `__post_init__` (below), or before:"
